WEBVTT


00:00:01.170 --> 00:00:04.740
Let DesignaKnit quickly space
your button holes for you.

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:08.200
Let's start with a basic cardigan sweater.

00:00:09.430 --> 00:00:11.500
Switch to Original Pattern Drafting.

00:00:13.090 --> 00:00:16.870
Select one of the front pieces
and zoom in on that front piece.

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:23.190
We want the first button hole, one
inch from the neck opening, and

00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:25.439
there will be a total of six buttons.

00:00:27.439 --> 00:00:31.130
I'm turning on my numbering, so we have
a point of reference to talk about here.

00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:35.950
Make sure that your rulers
are set to dimensions.

00:00:36.430 --> 00:00:37.960
In my case, inches.

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:45.850
Set the zero orientation to point number
six at the very edge of my neck opening.

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:52.240
Now I'm going to add my
top button one inch down.

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:56.890
I'll turn on the hairline cursor
to make things a little easier.

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:04.890
Select the plus symbol or the add tool
and place the point for the first button.

00:01:05.505 --> 00:01:10.335
Right now you're looking
at the lower corner.

00:01:10.335 --> 00:01:13.575
You can see that it's a little
difficult to place this exactly.

00:01:13.575 --> 00:01:18.825
I guess I could zoom in and
add point 7 but let's peek at

00:01:18.825 --> 00:01:20.955
the table and be more accurate.

00:01:21.555 --> 00:01:27.195
Click >piece >table and you can
see that point number seven is not

00:01:27.195 --> 00:01:29.625
exactly one inch down or in line.

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:35.640
So let's make it minus one and zero.

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:44.370
Guess what?

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:48.270
There are only two more steps to
position the other five buttons.

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:54.750
Click >alter >equal parts,
and click on the lower line

00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:56.640
between points seven and eight.

00:01:58.135 --> 00:02:03.205
When prompted, enter five for the
five remaining buttons and click okay.

00:02:04.785 --> 00:02:05.475
Voila.

00:02:05.535 --> 00:02:09.225
We have six evenly spaced
marks for our buttons.

00:02:09.824 --> 00:02:10.965
Let's confirm that.

00:02:11.385 --> 00:02:16.965
We'll use our measure tool and we'll find
out how many rows are between our buttons.

00:02:18.605 --> 00:02:20.795
Change the rulers to stitches and rows.

00:02:20.795 --> 00:02:26.015
This time, measure between 0.7 and eight.

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:30.960
Remember, we always measure clockwise
around the piece with this tool.

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:37.160
So DesignaKnit calculated
32 rows between my buttons.

00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:43.890
Well, this works if you're adding
a vertical band or a facing to your

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:48.390
sweater, but what if you're picking
up and knitting a band horizontally?

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:50.579
Simply rotate the piece.

00:02:55.520 --> 00:03:00.530
Click alter, rotate clockwise 90 degrees.

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:06.980
Now we can see that at my gauge
there are 20 stitches between the

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:11.660
buttons and you can easily calculate
your button holes accordingly.

